win32u, winex11: Clip a new window surface before its first flush, and clip an empty region out.

A window whose client surface goes offscreen gets a window surface again, and
a new window surface is white until painted. Two things let that white reach
the screen over the window's client surface:

  - update_surface_region() set the surface's shape before its clip. A
    changed shape flushes the whole surface, so the new surface was flushed
    in full with no clip at all, and the window turned white until its client
    surface was copied in again.

  - x11drv_surface_set_clip() took a region with no rectangles for the
    absence of a region and removed the clip. win32u passes NULL rects for
    that; an empty region means nothing of the surface is visible, as when a
    client surface covers the whole window. So every later flush painted
    white over the client surface too.

Set the clip first, and clip everything out for an empty region.

In Affinity, opening the first document makes the main window's Vulkan
surface go offscreen. A trace from the desktop where the whole window flashed
bright, then the canvas area: surface created at +15 ms, flushed in full at
+24 ms, clip set (empty) at +29 ms. With this, under a nested KWin, the
window's pixels and KWin's output show no white or black frame when the
document opens, and starting Affinity and showing dialogs stay free of
flashes.

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/window.c b/dlls/win32u/window.c
index 4b16446..92dc6e6 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -2118,8 +2118,10 @@ static void update_surface_region( HWND hwnd )
         if (win->dwExStyle & WS_EX_LAYOUTRTL) NtUserMirrorRgn( hwnd, shape );
         NtGdiDeleteObjectApp( region );
     }
-    window_surface_set_shape( win->surface, shape );
 
+    /* Clip before setting the shape: a changed shape flushes the whole surface,
+     * and a new surface is white until painted, so without its clip that flush
+     * covers what is clipped out -- client surfaces among it -- in white. */
     if (get_window_region( hwnd, TRUE, &region, &visible )) goto done;
     if (!region) window_surface_set_clip( win->surface, shape );
     else
@@ -2130,6 +2132,8 @@ static void update_surface_region( HWND hwnd )
         NtGdiDeleteObjectApp( region );
     }
 
+    window_surface_set_shape( win->surface, shape );
+
 done:
     if (shape) NtGdiDeleteObjectApp( shape );
     release_win_ptr( win );
diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
index 1b9c888..edc9017 100644
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
@@ -1776,8 +1776,15 @@ static void x11drv_surface_set_clip( struct window_surface *window_surface, cons
 
     TRACE( "surface %p, rects %p, count %u\n", surface, rects, count );
 
-    if (!count)
+    if (!rects)
         XSetClipMask( gdi_display, surface->gc, None );
+    else if (!count)
+    {
+        /* An empty region, not the absence of one: nothing of the surface is
+         * visible, typically because client surfaces cover all of it. */
+        XRectangle none = {0};
+        XSetClipRectangles( gdi_display, surface->gc, 0, 0, &none, 0, YXBanded );
+    }
     else if ((xrects = xrectangles_from_rects( rects, count )))
     {
         XSetClipRectangles( gdi_display, surface->gc, 0, 0, xrects, count, YXBanded );
